+\subsection{External Data Sources and Forecast Covariates}
+
+The transfer component has three base inputs: local precipitation from the PRISM Climate Group \citep{prism}, soil moisture from ECMWF ERA5-Land \citep{ecmwf, munozsabater2021era5land}, and a summary of broader climate conditions. Historical precipitation and soil moisture are extracted at the gauge location. Forecast-window inputs include Global Ensemble Forecast System (GEFS) precipitation and shallow soil-water forecasts, summarized as deterministic covariate paths. Precipitation enters through transfer predictors, including dry days; there is no separate precipitation occurrence or intensity model. ERA5-Land supplies a reanalysis-based land-state covariate with uncertainty, while USGS discharge supplies the verification target.
+
+Monthly climate and environmental indices from NOAA's Physical Sciences Laboratory \citep{noaa_climate_indices} provide the broader climate information: solar flux, Ni\~no 1+2, the Oceanic Ni\~no Index, the Southern Oscillation Index, the Western Hemisphere Warm Pool, global mean temperature, the Northern Oscillation Index, the Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation, and tropical South and North Atlantic indices. The series are interpolated to daily resolution and standardized. Their first generalized dynamic principal component (GDPC; \citealp{JSSv092c02}) explains 44.07\% of the index matrix's total variability and enters as a covariate. Figure~\ref{fig:covariates} displays the three base input series.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+\centering
+\centerline{\includegraphics[width=450pt]{covariate_context_precip_soil_gdpc.png}}
+\caption{
+Historical daily precipitation [mm], soil moisture [m$^3$/m$^3$], and first GDPC score over May 29, 1987 through December 25, 2022.}
+\label{fig:covariates}
+\end{figure}
+
+GloFAS and NWS provide the external discharge product families. The NWS source includes NOAA National Water Model (NWM) output \citep{cosgrove2024noaa}; the GloFAS system couples meteorological forcing to a global hydrological model \citep{harrigan2020glofas}. Each contributes a retrospective series for learning discrepancies from the USGS target and an ensemble for the forecast window. Figure~\ref{fig:retrospectives} displays their historical support for the December 25, 2022 origin. Exact source versions and extraction records accompany the model-ready inputs in the reproducibility archive \citep{deleon2026sanlorenzo_reproducibility}.
+
+These products also differ in spatial support. For gridded covariates and GloFAS discharge fields, the workflow extracts the cell or nearest valid river cell associated with the Big Trees gauge coordinates. For NWM streamflow, the workflow uses the corresponding model-output point or reach series and aggregates the latest available forecast issuances to daily member-specific forecast matrices. The PRISM precipitation path uses the 4 km product, while ERA5-Land soil moisture is extracted from its 0.1-degree gridded product. The GloFAS retrospective input uses version 2.1 for January 2021 and version 3.1 for the other origins. The NWM retrospective input uses version 2.1, with a version 3.0 extension beyond its coverage. The archive records these versions and the extraction policy for each origin \citep{deleon2026sanlorenzo_reproducibility}.
+
+The operational forecast products are used only after a cutoff date. The NWS ensemble has 7 members: 6 members provide medium-range forecasts up to 8.5 days, and one control member extends to 10 days. The GloFAS ensemble contains 51 members, all providing medium-range forecasts for up to 28 days. The products differ in source, forecast horizon, and update pattern. The model accommodates these differences through source-specific channels and lead-time availability.
